{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "06 Who can see what",
  "description": "Visibility tiers for minds and swarms, how personal minds stay hidden, local-only swarms that refuse outside model calls, and the policy gate on what minds may do. Lines were read at this repository's handover commit; if a line has moved, search the file for the step's function or symbol name.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "1. The rule in one paragraph",
      "description": "Minds and swarms are private, shared or public. Admins keep sight of everything (auditability). Every operator route checks what is in its path; lists and the live stream are filtered per person. A person who may not see something gets **not found**, never forbidden. Visibility governs who sees; policy governs what minds do."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/hive.py",
      "line": 323,
      "title": "2. New things are private",
      "description": "Every new mind and swarm gets an owner (the creator) and a visibility, `private` unless the caller chooses otherwise. `shared_with` may only name people (`user:...`), checked by `clean` (`hive/core/visibility.py` line 193)."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 205,
      "title": "3. Viewer.sees",
      "description": "The one test: admin sees all; public is seen by every signed-in person; the owner sees their own; shared adds the people listed. Rows from before this rule have no visibility and count as public."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 299,
      "title": "4. Routes check their path",
      "description": "`check_path` refuses a mind, swarm or goal in the route that this person may not see, as not found. Tour 03 shows it on the app action route."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 384,
      "title": "5. Lists are filtered",
      "description": "`GET /api/agents` (`hive/core/app.py` line 1229) passes the full list through here. Members get only rows they see; admins get the list minus other people's personal minds (step 9)."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 499,
      "title": "6. The live stream is filtered",
      "description": "Each event on the live socket goes through `event_visible` before it is sent (`hive/core/app.py` line 1957). Every event kind has a registered rule (scoped, public, admin and others); an unregistered kind is withheld. A scoped event goes only to people who see every mind, swarm and room it names."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 416,
      "title": "7. Ask content goes to the asker only",
      "description": "Ask frames, messages in an `ask:` conversation and Ask-scoped traces go only to the person who asked. This is checked **before** the admin bypass, so admins do not receive other people's live Ask content; they read turns through the Ask audit routes."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/personal.py",
      "line": 119,
      "title": "8. A personal mind",
      "description": "Each named person gets a personal mind in a private personal swarm, marked `personal_of`, created asleep, and made the swarm's coordinator. Its budget is 0: the person pays from their own allowance."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/visibility.py",
      "line": 312,
      "title": "9. Personal minds are hidden even from admins' default lists",
      "description": "`hidden_personal` finds other people's personal minds and swarms that this person could not see as an ordinary member. `hide_others_personal` (line 343) drops them from every admin default list. An admin sees them only with an explicit audit (`?include=personal`)."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/personal.py",
      "line": 100,
      "title": "10. Nobody else joins a personal swarm",
      "description": "A mind may enter a personal swarm only as that person's own personal mind. In the Ask, a personal mind answers only its own person (`hive/core/ask.py` line 312)."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/gateway.py",
      "line": 230,
      "title": "11. Local-only swarms",
      "description": "`HIVE_LOCAL_ONLY_SWARMS` lists swarms whose minds may use only local models. A call from such a swarm is refused (403 `egress_refused`) unless the provider is marked local and its address is a literal loopback IP with no credentials, query or fragment. A malformed list refuses every call."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/gateway.py",
      "line": 293,
      "title": "12. Where the local-only rule runs",
      "description": "In `estimate`, so before any budget check or reservation. It runs again in `chat` (line 343), `embeddings` (line 378), on the embeddings route (`hive/core/app.py` line 2150) and on the media route (`hive/core/media.py` line 496)."
    },
    {
      "file": "modules/hive/src/hive_commons.py",
      "line": 955,
      "title": "13. The gate, and what skips it",
      "description": "Before a native mind runs a skill, it posts the command to `/api/agent/authorize` (line 957; `hive/core/app.py` line 2679). It fails closed: if the hive cannot be asked, the command does not run. The gate is not universal yet. Line 955 returns before that call for every skill in `LOCAL_ALLOW` (line 932): `send`, `send-hive-to`, `wait`, `pin`, `query`, `remember`, `episodes`, `hive-publish`, `hive-query`, `hive-belief`, `hive-goals`, `hive-goal-claim`, `hive-goal-done`, `hive-goal-fail`, `hive-goal-create`, `hive-shares` and `hive-revision-part`. A deny or ask rule on these is never consulted for a native mind's loop (K10). And a skill that matches no rule and no default list is allowed (`hive/core/policy.py` line 219, K30). Universal gating is a launch requirement (K10, K30); see [FINDINGS.md](https://github.com/PhysixSNET/omegahive-schematics/blob/main/07-upgrade-path/FINDINGS.md)."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/policy.py",
      "line": 181,
      "title": "14. Policy.decide",
      "description": "Human-only skills are always denied. Some skills need an exact allow rule for that mind. Otherwise the most specific rule wins (mind, then swarm, then hive; an exact name beats a glob; then the most restrictive; then the newest). A hive skill not yet reviewed is never allowed by a glob. With no rule, defaults apply: allow, or ask for effects outside the mind."
    },
    {
      "file": "hive/core/policy.py",
      "line": 237,
      "title": "15. authorize and enforce",
      "description": "`authorize` turns the decision into allow, deny or pending. For `ask`, an approval is filed; once a person approves it, that exact command is allowed once. Bound coding operations are redeemed by the service for their exact binding instead. Routes that external members call directly use `enforce` (line 290), which applies the same rules."
    }
  ]
}
